      "text": "48 0.036 0.038 0.04 0.042 0.044 0.046 0.048 0.05 0.052 0.054 Moment of Interia of Bat: J (kg\u22c5 m2)\n\n\ud835\udc58\ud835\udc58\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f= 0.1 \ud835\udc58\ud835\udc58\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f\ud835\udc4f data\n\n\uf06e The Effect of Mass ( \ud835\udc74\ud835\udc74) on Maximum BBS Similar to the effect of MOI, the effect of Mass on BBS cannot be determined only by qualitative analysis. We make the quantitative analysis in the same procedure as MOI, only replacing \ud835\udf14\ud835\udf14\ud835\udc56\ud835\udc56 with \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc49\ud835\udc56\ud835\udc56, and \ud835\udc3d\ud835\udc3d with \ud835\udc40\ud835\udc40. From Figure 17, we easily obtain the similar result that the increase of \ud835\udc40\ud835\udc40 (limited in possible field of variation) leads to higher maximum BBS. What\u2019s more,\n\nwith the increase of mass.\n\nEspecially, we draw the swinging bat speed \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc49\ud835\udc56\ud835\udc56 and maximum BBS \ud835\udc63\ud835\udc63\ud835\udc56\ud835\udc56 with respect to mass \ud835\udc40\ud835\udc40 in the same figure, as Figure 18 shows. Comparing with\n\ndata obtained from calculation of our model exactly match that experimental data, which proves the correctness of our model.\n\n36\n\n34\n\nSwinging Bat Speed: Vi (m/s) 32\n\n30\n\n28\n\n26\n\n24\n\n22 0 0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 1 1.2 1.4 Mass of Corked Bat: M (kg)\n\n0.3\n\n0.28\n\nLocation of SweetSpot: bSweetSpot 0.26\n\n0.24\n\n0.22\n\n0.2\n\n0.18\n\n0.16\n\n0.14\n\n0.12 0 0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 1 1.2 1.4 Mass of Corked Bat: M (kg) 55\n\n50\n\n45\n\n40 Maximum BBS: Vf,max 35\n\n30\n\n25\n\n20\n\n15\n\n10\n\n5 0 0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 1 1.2 1.4 Mass of Corked Bat: M (kg)\n\n55\n\n50\n\n45 Batted-Ball Speed: vf 40\n\n35 Speed (m/s) 30\n\n25 Swinging Bat Speed: Vi 20\n\n15\n\n10\n\n5 0 0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 1 1.2 1.4 Mass of Corked Bat: M (kg)\n\nbat mass \ud835\udc4